WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.301
Batch Normalization.

2
00:00:01.581 --> 00:00:10.989
Ioffe and Szegedy (2015) proposed a blunter instrument: if you want pre-activations to be roughly standard normal, standardize them.

3
00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:23.899
For each feature, compute the mean and variance over the current minibatch, subtract and divide, and then apply a learned scale gamma and shift beta, so the network can undo the normalization wherever that helps.

4
00:00:24.179 --> 00:00:27.379
The layer sits between a linear layer and its nonlinearity.

5
00:00:27.659 --> 00:00:32.160
Several consequences follow, and the lecture covers each: Redundant bias.

6
00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:39.523
Mean subtraction removes any constant the previous layer added, so its bias does nothing and is usually disabled.

7
00:00:39.803 --> 00:00:41.360
Beta takes over the job.

8
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:49.853
You’ll see this as bias=False before BatchNorm in the ResNet code the lecture reads at 01:04:50.

9
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:51.029
Two modes.

10
00:00:51.309 --> 00:00:55.320
At inference there may be one example, so batch statistics don’t exist.

11
00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:02.619
BatchNorm keeps running averages of the mean and variance during training (PyTorch’s default momentum is 0.

12
00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:04.819
1) and uses them at inference.

13
00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.680
Forgetting to switch modes is a classic bug.

14
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.005
Batch coupling.

15
00:01:09.285 --> 00:01:13.616
During training each example’s output depends on the others in its batch.

16
00:01:13.896 --> 00:01:20.019
That jitter acts as a regularizer, but it also makes behavior depend on batch size and composition.

17
00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:29.216
The lecture’s description links Wu and Johnson’s “Rethinking Batch in BatchNorm” (2021) for the practical problems this causes.

18
00:01:29.496 --> 00:01:43.939
A detail you’ll meet next lesson: PyTorch’s BatchNorm1d uses the biased variance (divide by n) to normalize during training, but the unbiased estimate (divide by n minus 1) for the running variance.

19
00:01:44.219 --> 00:01:48.165
The Backprop Ninja lecture digresses on exactly this mismatch.
